WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.687
Back in the agent-loop episode we said instructions stack instead of replacing each other.

2
00:00:02.687 --> 00:00:06.000
Now let's make that concrete with a real project in front of us — global, project, and chat, all three at once.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.806
First, a global instruction in Settings — this applies everywhere, in every project and every chat.

4
00:00:12.806 --> 00:00:17.000
We'll set it to something simple: always reply in UK English.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:24.376
Second, a project instruction, set on the Acme project itself: always mention the account manager's name in a reply.

6
00:00:24.376 --> 00:00:28.000
This only applies inside Acme — Brightside never sees it.

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.114
Third, a chat instruction, set on one specific conversation inside Acme: keep replies under three sentences.

8
00:00:35.114 --> 00:00:39.000
That's the narrowest layer — it only touches this one chat.

9
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.100
Send a message and look at the reply: it's in UK English, it names the account manager, and it's three sentences or fewer.

10
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:52.000
All three layers showed up at once, because none of them replaced any other — they stacked, exactly like the agent-loop episode described.

11
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.348
Two diagnoses worth memorizing.

12
00:00:53.348 --> 00:00:55.261
Facts from one project leaking into another?

13
00:00:55.261 --> 00:00:58.304
Check Settings, Memory — the scope may be set wider than project-only.

14
00:00:58.304 --> 00:01:00.478
A chat not picking up project instructions at all?

15
00:01:00.478 --> 00:01:05.000
It simply isn't inside the project yet — drag it in, or start it from the project's own new-chat button.

16
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Instructions are one way to shape a reply.

17
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Next, Module 9 covers a bigger one — giving the model your own documents to draw on.

